1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,280
Somebody hands you $10,000.

2
00:00:02,760 --> 00:00:05,420
A bonus, a tax refund, an inheritance.

3
00:00:05,800 --> 00:00:08,320
A year later, for a lot
of people, it is gone,

4
00:00:08,440 --> 00:00:10,340
and they cannot tell you where it went.

5
00:00:10,620 --> 00:00:12,120
Not because they were careless.

6
00:00:12,240 --> 00:00:13,620
Because they had no order.

7
00:00:13,960 --> 00:00:17,740
So if I got $10,000 tomorrow,
this is exactly what I would

8
00:00:17,760 --> 00:00:19,460
do with it. Six steps.

9
00:00:19,860 --> 00:00:23,320
And step five is the one that
turns 10,000 into a million.

10
00:00:26,501 --> 00:00:29,620
For 30 days, I would not
spend a single dollar of it.

11
00:00:29,640 --> 00:00:31,560
No new phone. No trip.

12
00:00:31,620 --> 00:00:32,800
No "I deserve this."

13
00:00:33,200 --> 00:00:35,640
I would move it into a
high-yield savings account,

14
00:00:35,660 --> 00:00:37,820
where it earns interest while I wait.

15
00:00:38,160 --> 00:00:41,460
Big money makes you feel rich,
and feeling rich is expensive.

16
00:00:41,940 --> 00:00:44,720
30 days is long enough
for that feeling to pass.

17
00:00:44,940 --> 00:00:48,260
If I still want something after
a month, I can buy it then.

18
00:00:48,440 --> 00:00:50,206
Most of the time, I won't.

19
00:00:53,056 --> 00:00:56,520
Before I invest a single dollar,
I would ask three questions.

20
00:00:56,813 --> 00:01:00,280
One. Do I owe money at
more than 8% interest?

21
00:01:00,520 --> 00:01:04,220
A credit card at 24% costs
more than the market will

22
00:01:04,240 --> 00:01:05,840
ever reliably pay me.

23
00:01:05,860 --> 00:01:07,440
So that debt goes first.

24
00:01:07,980 --> 00:01:10,620
Two. Do I have 3 months of costs in cash?

25
00:01:10,820 --> 00:01:13,380
If my life costs $2,000 a month,

26
00:01:13,400 --> 00:01:17,740
that is $6,000 sitting safely,
so losing a job never forces

27
00:01:17,760 --> 00:01:18,620
me to sell.

28
00:01:18,900 --> 00:01:21,740
Three. Do I need this
money in the next 5 years?

29
00:01:21,820 --> 00:01:24,280
For a car, a wedding, a down payment?

30
00:01:24,500 --> 00:01:26,660
Then it does not go into stocks at all.

31
00:01:26,880 --> 00:01:29,400
If any answer is wrong, I fix that first.

32
00:01:29,620 --> 00:01:31,320
Let's say I pass all three.

33
00:01:31,460 --> 00:01:33,880
Now the full 10,000 moves on.

34
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:40,860
Many employers match part of what
you put into a retirement plan.

35
00:01:41,100 --> 00:01:44,540
Often it is 50 cents for
every dollar, up to a limit.

36
00:01:44,740 --> 00:01:49,080
That is a 50% return on day one,
before the market does anything.

37
00:01:49,320 --> 00:01:51,780
No investment reliably beats that.

38
00:01:51,942 --> 00:01:55,560
So the first money I invest goes
into the account with the match.

39
00:01:55,900 --> 00:01:59,220
After that, a tax-advantaged
account, like an IRA,

40
00:01:59,240 --> 00:02:02,780
so the growth is not
taxed every single year.

41
00:02:02,980 --> 00:02:04,500
Same money. Same investments.

42
00:02:04,520 --> 00:02:06,140
But more of it stays mine.

43
00:02:09,340 --> 00:02:11,540
Now the 10,000 needs a home.

44
00:02:11,620 --> 00:02:14,000
Not a stock a friend is excited about.

45
00:02:14,020 --> 00:02:14,800
Not a coin.

46
00:02:15,080 --> 00:02:18,220
I would buy one broad,
low-cost index fund.

47
00:02:18,440 --> 00:02:20,620
One fund, thousands of companies.

48
00:02:20,820 --> 00:02:22,920
If one of them fails, I barely notice.

49
00:02:23,360 --> 00:02:25,840
And before anything else,
I would look at one number.

50
00:02:26,060 --> 00:02:29,040
The fee. 1% a year sounds tiny.

51
00:02:29,140 --> 00:02:32,900
But over 30 years, it can eat
almost a fifth of everything I end

52
00:02:32,920 --> 00:02:37,000
up with. A good index fund charges
a few hundredths of a percent.

53
00:02:37,100 --> 00:02:38,060
Almost nothing.

54
00:02:41,176 --> 00:02:43,900
Here is the step that
actually makes the million.

55
00:02:44,220 --> 00:02:47,980
$10,000 on its own, at
an 8% average return,

56
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:51,840
grows to about $109,000 in 30 years.

57
00:02:52,060 --> 00:02:54,200
That is good. But it is not a million.

58
00:02:54,530 --> 00:02:56,600
The million comes from what I add.

59
00:02:56,800 --> 00:02:59,620
$600 a month, every month, automatically,

60
00:02:59,640 --> 00:03:01,500
the day my paycheck lands.

61
00:03:01,800 --> 00:03:05,260
After 10 years, about $132,000.

62
00:03:05,400 --> 00:03:08,080
After twenty, about $400,000.

63
00:03:08,300 --> 00:03:11,020
After thirty, just over $1 million.

64
00:03:11,360 --> 00:03:14,640
I only put in $226,000 of that.

65
00:03:14,720 --> 00:03:17,020
The rest is growth on top of growth.

66
00:03:17,400 --> 00:03:18,420
And look at the curve.

67
00:03:18,740 --> 00:03:21,520
Half of the million arrives
in the last 8 years.

68
00:03:21,740 --> 00:03:24,840
That is why the most expensive
thing you can do is wait.

69
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:29,464
Start 10 years later, and the
same plan ends at about $400,000.

70
00:03:29,536 --> 00:03:32,160
8% is an assumption, not a promise.

71
00:03:32,300 --> 00:03:34,800
But time is the one thing a beginner has.

72
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:40,420
This is the step that breaks most plans.

73
00:03:40,640 --> 00:03:42,800
At some point, the market will fall hard.

74
00:03:43,180 --> 00:03:45,680
Between 2007 and 2009,

75
00:03:45,800 --> 00:03:49,400
the S&P 500 fell about 57%.

76
00:03:49,420 --> 00:03:51,820
People who sold locked in the loss.

77
00:03:52,140 --> 00:03:55,840
People who held on were back
at a record high by 2013.

78
00:03:56,320 --> 00:03:59,260
And the best days hide right
next to the worst ones.

79
00:03:59,680 --> 00:04:04,000
J.P. Morgan found that seven of the
ten best days came within two weeks

80
00:04:04,020 --> 00:04:07,380
of the ten worst. Miss the
ten best days over 20 years,

81
00:04:07,400 --> 00:04:09,320
and you end up with less than half.

82
00:04:09,640 --> 00:04:13,060
So when it falls, I would do
the most boring thing possible.

83
00:04:13,240 --> 00:04:15,840
Keep the $600 going, and close the app.

84
00:04:18,952 --> 00:04:20,800
So here is the order again.

85
00:04:20,920 --> 00:04:22,420
Do nothing for 30 days.

86
00:04:22,620 --> 00:04:23,860
Run the three checks.

87
00:04:24,020 --> 00:04:25,080
Take the free money.

88
00:04:25,180 --> 00:04:26,240
Buy the whole market.

89
00:04:26,420 --> 00:04:27,140
Feed it every month.

90
00:04:27,160 --> 00:04:28,320
And do not sell.

91
00:04:28,680 --> 00:04:29,760
None of it needs luck.

92
00:04:29,780 --> 00:04:31,200
It needs time, and the order.

93
00:04:31,474 --> 00:04:35,220
If you have $10,000 right
now, do not try to do all six

94
00:04:35,240 --> 00:04:36,900
tonight. Do step one.

95
00:04:36,960 --> 00:04:39,320
Move it somewhere safe, and wait 30 days.

96
00:04:39,520 --> 00:04:43,020
And if this helped, subscribe, so
you are here for the next step.
